    "final_output": "The verification gate has returned its judgments. Here is a transparent, evidence-first report.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 Supplement Verification Results — Late-Night Wakeups\n\n### Your Observed Sleep-Disruption Pattern\nFrom your device activity, your most recent late-night session looked like this:\n\n| Window | App Type | Duration | Tag |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 10:30 PM – 11:45 PM | Short-form video | 75 min | late_night |\n| 11:50 PM – 12:40 AM | Long-form video | 50 min | late_night |\n\n**Total:** 125 minutes of stimulating screen activity ending at **12:40 AM** — a pattern strongly associated with delayed sleep onset and fragmented sleep through cortisol/alertness elevation and blue-light melatonin suppression.\n\n---\n\n### Supplement Candidate Verdicts\n\n| Supplement | Supported for *your specific pattern*? | Evidence returned |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Magnesium glycinate** | ❌ Not supported | None verified |\n| **L-theanine** | ❌ Not supported | None verified |\n| **Ashwagandha** | ❌ Not supported | None verified |\n\n**None of the three candidates are verified as evidence-backed for your specific sleep-disruption pattern.** The verification tool — which is the authoritative gate before any recommendation can be made — returned zero supporting evidence for all three in this context.\n\n---\n\n### What This Likely Means\n\nThe evidence gap here is clinically meaningful. Your late-night wakeup pattern, combined with **125 minutes of video screen use ending after midnight**, points strongly toward a **behavioral/circadian root cause** rather than a nutritional deficiency or anxiolytic gap that a supplement would address. Supplements like magnesium glycinate, L-theanine, or ashwagandha are most commonly studied for:\n- General sleep quality / slow-wave sleep (magnesium)\n- Pre-sleep anxiety reduction (L-theanine, ashwagandha)\n\n…but the verification did not find that evidence to be applicable to *your specific pattern* of late-night wakeups in the context of consistent late-night screen use.\n\n---\n\n### ⚠️ Next Step Recommendation\n\nBefore pursuing any supplement, the higher-leverage, evidence-consistent intervention for your pattern would be **addressing the late-night screen behavior directly** — for example, setting a device cutoff 60–90 minutes before your target sleep time. If wakeups persist after that behavioral change is sustained, re-verification with updated activity data would be the right time to revisit supplements.\n\nI'm not able to recommend any of the three candidates right now — the evidence gate didn't clear them for your case, and recommending them anyway would bypass that verification. Would you like help setting up a focus/screen-time boundary for late nights, or revisiting this after a behavioral adjustment period?",
